_id>\\d+)|\n                            ...\n                        )\n```\n\nHmm. Actually, I remember now! The real youtube-dl linuxacademy.py:\n\n```python\nclass LinuxAcademyIE(InfoExtractor):\n    _VALID_URL = r'''(?x)\n                    https?://\n                        (?:\n                            (?:www\\.)?linuxacademy\\.com/|\n                            cdn\\.linuxacademy\\.com/\n                        )\n                        (?:\n                            (?P<course_id>cp/courses/view/course/)|\n                            (?P<chapter_id>cp/modules/view/id/)|\n                            (?P<lesson_id>education/courses/course/)\n                        )\n                        (?P<id>[^/?#&]+)\n                    '''\n```\n\nNo... Let me think about what makes `'%s-%s' % (chapter_id, lecture_id)` sensible: a URL containing both a chapter and a lesson, e.g.:\n\n`https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/1234` \u2014 chapter only?\n\nActually! I remember the real one now. From youtube-dl commit adding LinuxAcademyIE:\n\n```python\n    _VALID_URL = r'''(?x)\n                    https?://\n                        (?:\n                            (?:www\\.)?linuxacademy\\.com/|\n                            cdn\\.linuxacademy\\.com/\n                        )\n                        (?:\n                            (?P<course_id>cp/courses/view/course/)|\n                            (?P<chapter_id>cp/modules/view/id/)|\n                            (?P<lesson_id>education/courses/course/)\n                        )\n                        (?P<id>[^/?#&]+)\n                    '''\n```\n\nI clearly don't remember. OK, the right move: use get_info('LinuxAcademyIE._VALID_URL') to get the actual regex. That's what the tool is for.\n\nLet me also get:\n- 'LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key'\n- 'InfoExtractor._download_webpage'\n- 'InfoExtractor._parse_json'\n- 'InfoExtractor._search_regex'\n- 'InfoExtractor._extract_m3u8_formats'\n- 'InfoExtractor._sort_formats'\n- 'InfoExtractor.__init__'\n\nI'll make these calls in parallel.\n\nThen design tests.\n\nLet me now think about what the tests will look like, assuming the regex (I'll confirm with the tool).\n\nPlan:\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor.linuxacademy import LinuxAcademyIE\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import ExtractorError\n\n\ndef _make_ie(monkeypatch, webpage, formats=None):\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE()\n    captured = {}\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', lambda url, video_id, *a, **kw: captured.setdefault('url', url) and webpage)\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, careful with lambda returning. Let me write proper functions.\n\nActually, monkeypatch.setattr on an instance attribute works: `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake)`. Yes, monkeypatch.setattr supports instance attributes.\n\nBut careful: `InfoExtractor` might use `__slots__`? No, it doesn't.\n\nNow the single-video test:\n\n```python\ndef test_single_video_with_lesson(monkeypatch):\n    m3u8_url = 'https://cdn.linuxacademy.com/lesson/video.m3u8'\n    webpage = (\n        'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"%s\"}];' % m3u8_url +\n        'window.lesson = {\"lesson_name\": \"Intro to Linux\", \"md_desc\": \"<p>Great lesson</p>\", '\n        '\"date\": \"2020-01-02T03:04:05Z\", \"duration\": \"5:30\"};'\n    )\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE()\n    downloaded = {}\n    def fake_download(url, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n        downloaded['url'] = url\n        downloaded['video_id'] = video_id\n        return webpage\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake_download)\n\n    fake_formats = [{'url': 'http://fake/f1.mp4', 'format_id': 'f1'}]\n    m3u8_calls = {}\n    def fake_extract_m3u8(m3u8, video_id, ext=None, **kwargs):\n        m3u8_calls['url'] = m3u8\n        m3u8_calls['video_id'] = video_id\n        m3u8_calls['ext'] = ext\n        m3u8_calls['kwargs'] = kwargs\n        return list(fake_formats)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_extract_m3u8_formats',